comments.body:
Fresh review against `origin/main` at `2d63119a`: adoption by reference is the right direction, but widening the scope sentence and adding `op: dispatch` are not sufficient. Expansion's idempotency apparatus is not parameter-neutral.

The common fingerprint currently requires `source` to be a Mission resolved from `subject_token`, uses `(client, creation_request_id)` as its reservation key, carries token-exchange-specific members such as `requested_token_type`, and defines recovery around deferred/interactive creation and expiring delivery credentials. Template has no source Mission or `subject_token`; its stable source is the Mission Template, its identifier is `dispatch_event_id`, and its request/response/revalidation conditions differ.

Please add an explicit adopter binding, not only an adopter name:

- identifier and reservation key: `(authenticated client, dispatch_event_id)`;
- fingerprint domain: `op: dispatch`;
- source binding: either generalize `source` to the operation's source authority object and set it to `template_id`, or define a dispatch-specific `template` member without pretending it is a Mission ID;
- exact included inputs: `template_id`, parsed `mission_intent`/evidence, optional proposal, verified client and `cnf`, plus every parameter affecting approval/output/side effects; state which token-exchange-only members are absent;
- atomic reservation with the dispatched Mission and every creation-side effect;
- retry revalidation of Dispatcher eligibility, recorded `cnf`, and the applicable template/version state;
- recovery as delivery of the same Mission, including what happens after the original access token expires.

Generalize the adoption clause carefully: continuation already adopts it for creation of a refresh-token family, which is not a Mission. “Creation exchanges explicitly profiled by adopters” is more accurate than only “Mission-creating exchanges.” Existing expansion/child vectors must remain byte-identical.

The mutual `max_derivations` cross-references are useful and should land. They connect continuation's one-family counting rule to child delegation's per-Mission, non-subtree counter warning without changing either rule.

Evidence: [common apparatus and fingerprint](https://github.com/mcguinness/mission-bound-authorization/blob/2d63119a16c29baa80106d8e6cd4b6cae00dfd29/draft-mcguinness-oauth-mission-expansion.md#L1132-L1266), [Template dispatch identifier](https://github.com/mcguinness/mission-bound-authorization/blob/2d63119a16c29baa80106d8e6cd4b6cae00dfd29/draft-mcguinness-oauth-mission-template.md#L598-L613), and [Continuation adopter mapping](https://github.com/mcguinness/mission-bound-authorization/blob/2d63119a16c29baa80106d8e6cd4b6cae00dfd29/draft-mcguinness-oauth-mission-continuation.md#L360-L405).

Disposition: approve adoption by reference with a complete dispatch binding; the current three-line sketch would leave key fingerprint and recovery semantics undefined.

Fresh review against `main` at `b4e35419`.

**Disposition: revise and split. Ship maturity honesty now; move navigation to a separate reader-facing issue.**

The problem is real, and the numbers in the issue are already stale. The current manifests contain 41 documents (`23 stable`, `12 experimental`, `3 sketch`, `3 informational`) and 460 conformance rows (`89 tested`, `39 partial`, `332 todo`). Runtime is 28/18/71, AuthZEN 13/2/63, and Runtime Evidence 6/1/82. More importantly, there is a direct semantic contradiction: `oauth-mission-approval` is labeled `stable` in the family manifest/README while its Status section says it is “not yet a stable interface” because its deferred-token substrate is early ([Status text](https://github.com/mcguinness/mission-bound-authorization/blob/b4e35419abc794b06afd3708ab851aee71670624/draft-mcguinness-oauth-mission-approval.md#L149-L158)). The current disclaimer that family maturity is not standards status does not cure that contradiction ([README maturity explanation](https://github.com/mcguinness/mission-bound-authorization/blob/b4e35419abc794b06afd3708ab851aee71670624/README.md#L60-L74)).

Do not adopt `core-candidate` and `companion-candidate` as maturity values. Core/companion is an architectural role; candidate/experimental/sketch is maturity. Combining the axes makes a companion impossible to mature without renaming its category and encourages readers to interpret “core-candidate” as more mature than “companion-candidate.”

Recommended data model:

- `role` or existing group/package: core, adapter/binding, companion, guide;
- `spec_maturity`: `candidate`, `experimental`, `sketch`, or `not_applicable` for non-protocol guides;
- standards category/status: sourced from document front matter and external dependency metadata;
- implementation/conformance: derived from the conformance manifest per spec and named profile;
- maintenance class/owner: keep the existing separate axis.

Likewise, `research-note` is not a replacement for `informational`: one describes purpose/category, the other suggests immaturity. An informational architecture or security guide can be mature. Preserve `informational`/guide as document role/category and make protocol maturity N/A where appropriate.

A `candidate` gate should require:

1. complete normative-requirement inventory for the claimed interface;
2. no known internal contradiction or unresolved `decide` issue affecting that interface;
3. a named interoperability/conformance floor and representative passing implementation evidence;
4. examples/vectors and successful build/manifest validation;
5. explicit disclosure of unstable external normative dependencies and their effect.

Do not gate candidate status on a raw percentage of optional `SHOULD`/capability rows. Coverage is implementation evidence, not specification semantics. Show it beside maturity rather than encoding it into the same word.

Extend validation beyond README↔manifest equality. A candidate document must not contain Status prose saying it is unstable; dependency stability needs structured data; and generated README/catalog views should derive all counts so issue text cannot drift immediately.

The navigation half should be split. The user-facing failure reported repeatedly is that the README remains long and duplicates adoption views, while the verbs are the distinctive, understandable spine. That supports making verbs the public front door and moving the full zone/track/group/package matrix to a generated linked catalog or collapsed reference. But that is a reader-information architecture decision and a reversal of a recent storefront choice; it should be tested and reviewed independently from a manifest enum migration. Mixing them makes either change harder to assess or revert.

Concrete issue split:

- keep #707 for the maturity axes, rename, gates, generated counts, and consistency validator;
- open a navigation issue whose acceptance test is one short README path: choose a verb/outcome, choose a binding, see the minimum package, then follow a link for the full catalog. Remove duplicate adoption tables rather than merely demoting their terminology.

That gives WG reviewers honest maturity language without using implementation incompleteness as a proxy for protocol quality, and gives the README problem its own measurable reader outcome.

pulls.body:
Implements course (a) from the ruling on #424: clarify the core approval-authentication-floor passage so the normative home is the deployment's published statement, with the Mission Deployment Profile reduced to informative naming. #424 stays parked.

The ruling's five normative consequences, verified against the diff:

- the published-floor MUST remains;
- the statement's applicable deployment scope is explicit;
- satisfaction is the conjunction of the floor and, when present, `controls.acr`, under the deployment's policy mapping, with no global ordering of `acr` values implied (the old "raise but never lower" ordering language is removed);
- the architecture reference is informative terminology, phrased in the document's existing citation idiom (mirrors the Mission Assurance Levels sentence at line 341) rather than the ruling's literal draft sentence;
- no new wire member, metadata, IANA registration, or schema.

One passage changed, references untouched (`architecture` was already informative). `make draft-mcguinness-oauth-mission.txt` builds clean; rendered text flows into the following paragraph without downstream edits.

Core is the published editor's draft; this PR is the reviewable change the #424 ruling authorized. Independent of the other review PRs; merge in any order.
